Project Archimedes: A Self-Assembling Modular Orbital Habitat Based on Autonomous Small-Satellite Swarms

Mathematical Foundations, Architecture, and Feasibility

Doctoral Dissertation — Final Version

---

Abstract

The construction of large pressurised habitats in low Earth orbit has, since the dawn of the space age, followed a single paradigm: fabricate large, pre‑integrated modules on Earth, launch them individually on heavy‑lift vehicles, and assemble them in orbit through human extravehicular activity. This approach imposes severe constraints on module dimensions, launch reliability, and cost, and fundamentally limits the scalability of orbital infrastructure. This dissertation presents a complete, mathematically rigorous, and architecturally closed framework—Project Archimedes—for a radically different approach: the autonomous in‑orbit assembly of a 400 m³ human‑rated habitat from approximately 500 small, identical, intelligent tiles (each ≤12 U CubeSat, ≤12.5 kg) that navigate, dock, and reconfigure themselves under the guidance of a hierarchical artificial intelligence.

The work makes five principal contributions. First, it develops a formal graph‑theoretic model of a swarm‑assembled structure, in which the habitat is represented as a time‑varying dynamic graph with separate mechanical, communication, and fluid‑transfer topologies. Second, it establishes a co‑design methodology for task assignment and collision‑free trajectory generation, employing a Consensus‑Based Bundle Algorithm (CBBA) with barrier‑augmented costs and sequential convex programming, with a full convergence proof provided. Third, the stochastic nature of assembly is rigorously captured as a Decentralised Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (Dec‑POMDP), solved via a Dec‑MCTS‑SP planner with a proven probabilistic completeness guarantee. Fourth, a suite of nonsmooth control barrier functions is derived that maintains algebraic connectivity of the communication graph under random tile failure and stabilises electromagnetic docking across the full LEO temperature envelope. Fifth, the mathematical results are translated into a complete hardware architecture with closed mass, power, propellant, and economic budgets, demonstrating that the entire programme—from first launch to crewed occupancy—can be realised for under $200 million, approximately 0.5% of the International Space Station's cost for comparable habitable volume.

The dissertation is a work of pure theory and design specification. Its contribution is a self‑contained, mathematically verified foundation upon which future simulation studies, hardware prototyping, and orbital flight experiments can be built.

