Chapter 2: Literature Review and Competitive Landscape

2.1 Modular Space Structures and the ISS Legacy

The International Space Station (ISS) is the archetypal modular orbital assembly. Over approximately 40 dedicated launches, modules of 10–20 tonnes each—Zarya, Unity, Zvezda, Destiny, Harmony, Columbus, Kibo, and others—were mated in a carefully choreographed sequence using the Space Shuttle's robotic arm and extensive astronaut EVA (Kitmacher, 2006). The ISS's Common Berthing Mechanism provides a standardised mechanical, power, and data interface, and the station has proven that on‑orbit assembly of large pressurised volumes is feasible. However, the ISS architecture also illustrates the fundamental constraints of the monolithic‑module paradigm: each module is a multi‑billion‑dollar, bespoke structure; module diameter is capped at approximately 4.5 m by the launch vehicle fairing; and the programme's total cost exceeded $150 billion (NASA OIG, 2018).

2.2 Self‑Assembling and Reconfigurable Space Systems

The ESA Advanced Concepts Team (ACT) has studied self‑configuring modular cubes for space applications, formulating the problem as a graph‑based self‑assembly task in which identical modules use local rules to achieve a target configuration. Their work specifically identifies that decentralised control is possible only if the communication graph remains connected, a finding that directly motivates our emphasis on algebraic connectivity maintenance.

NASA's ARMADAS (Automated Reconfigurable Mission Adaptive Digital Assembly Systems) programme has demonstrated the assembly of large truss structures by inchworm‑like walking robots in ground tests and is targeting in‑space demonstrations. ARMADAS proves that kilometre‑scale support frameworks can be built from small mass‑produced voxels, but does not address pressurised volumes or fluid handling.

2.3 TESSERAE: The Closest Existing Concept

The TESSERAE (Tessellated Electromagnetic Space Structures for the Exploration of Reconfigurable, Adaptive Environments) concept, invented by Dr. Ariel Ekblaw during her doctoral research at the MIT Media Lab, is the closest existing work to Project Archimedes. TESSERAE tiles are flat‑packed, self‑assembling hexagons or pentagons that use custom electropermanent magnets (EPMs) to dock and form geodesic shells in microgravity.

TESSERAE has achieved notable flight heritage: parabolic flight testing (2017), Blue Origin New Shepard suborbital flight (2019), two ISS orbital demonstrations (CRS‑20, 2020; Ax‑1, 2022), and a planned 32‑tile assembly test on the ISS in 2026. The technology is now being commercialised by Rendezvous Robotics, which raised $3 million in pre‑seed funding in September 2025. Ekblaw has published the underlying concept and initial test results, and the Aurelia Institute has built a full‑scale mockup of a TESSERAE habitat displayed at the Museum of Flight in Seattle.

However, TESSERAE has three publicly acknowledged limitations that Archimedes directly addresses: (1) the tiles are not airtight and no flight‑tested sealing solution exists; (2) the tiles currently lack propulsion, relying solely on magnetic attraction within a containment structure; and (3) the published work on TESSERAE does not include a formal mathematical framework for stochastic assembly planning, algebraic connectivity maintenance, or temperature‑compensated docking stability. The present dissertation provides these missing theoretical foundations.

2.4 Swarm Robotics and Distributed Assignment

The Consensus‑Based Bundle Algorithm (CBBA), introduced by Choi et al. (2009), provides provable convergence and 50% optimality for distributed task allocation when the score function satisfies the condition of diminishing marginal gain (DMG). Recent work has extended CBBA to handle budget constraints, dynamic task sets, and heterogeneous agent capabilities. Archimedes adopts CBBA as its assignment backbone and augments the score function with algebraic connectivity penalties and collision‑avoidance barrier terms.

2.5 Algebraic Connectivity and Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions

The algebraic connectivity of a graph, $\lambda_2(L)$, is the second smallest eigenvalue of its Laplacian and characterises how well‑connected the graph is (Fiedler, 1973). Ong et al. (2023) developed a nonsmooth control barrier function framework that uses $\lambda_2$ as a CBF to maintain connectivity in multi‑robot systems, critically embracing its nonsmooth nature via the Clarke generalised gradient. Archimedes adopts and extends this framework to the space assembly domain, adding temperature‑dependent terms to the Lyapunov function for electromagnetic docking.

2.6 In‑Orbit Welding

ThinkOrbital, in collaboration with TWI, achieved the world's first autonomous electron beam weld in space on a SpaceX Falcon 9 mission in May 2024. The system used a compact, self‑contained electron beam welding system, with X‑ray cameras for in‑situ seam inspection. This milestone proves that high‑quality structural joints can be created autonomously in the vacuum of space—a capability that Archimedes leverages for truss rigidisation.

2.7 Identified Gaps and Contributions

No existing work simultaneously addresses all of the following: a pressurised, human‑rated volume assembled entirely from sub‑15 kg tiles; a complete mathematical treatment including stochastic docking, thermal effects on electromagnets, and algebraic connectivity constraints under failure; a co‑design of distributed assignment and trajectory generation for hundreds of free‑flying tiles with convergence guarantees; a Dec‑POMDP formulation with decentralised belief‑space planning under realistic sensor degradation; and a closed architecture specification that resolves mass, power, propellant, and economic budgets within current technology constraints. Project Archimedes is the first framework to integrate all these elements.

