Chapter 4: Architecture and System Design

4.1 AI Hierarchy: Architect, Foreman, Mason

The system intelligence is organised in three tiers.

Level 1 — Architect (Strategic AI). A fine‑tuned Llama‑3 large language model hosted on the Nucleus module. It receives human‑language goals ("assemble a 400 m³ habitat ring"), retrieves engineering constraints via retrieval‑augmented generation, and decomposes the goal into a sequence of partial target graphs $\mathcal{G}_0^* \subset \mathcal{G}_1^* \subset \cdots \subset \mathcal{G}_{\text{final}}^*$. Every generated plan passes through the SPIN model checker, which verifies deadlock freedom, collision avoidance, power‑loop invariants, and $\lambda_2 \ge \lambda_{\min}$ at all stages. Plans that fail verification are automatically rejected; the system reverts to a pre‑verified graph‑grammar scaffold. The semantic gap between natural language and formal graph grammars is acknowledged and scoped as post‑doctoral future work—for the purposes of this dissertation, five hand‑coded assembly sequences demonstrate that the SPIN verifier correctly accepts safe plans and rejects unsafe ones.

Level 2 — Foreman (Orchestration AI). Executes CBBA assignment and SCP trajectory generation every 60 seconds, maintains a digital twin of the entire swarm for Dec‑MCTS‑SP belief updates, and replans on any tile failure. All issued commands are local and unambiguous.

Level 3 — Mason (Edge Autonomy). Each tile runs a lightweight policy network distilled from the Foreman. It performs visual/UWB sensor fusion (Parikh et al., 2024), 6‑DOF EM dipole control, thruster allocation, and local CBF‑QP execution at 10 Hz. If communication with the Foreman is lost for more than 300 s, the Mason transitions to safe‑hold mode.

A supervisory interface (Level 1.5) allows the human crew to query the swarm state via natural language and a 3D digital‑twin visualisation. Crew can issue high‑level directives such as "pause all docking for 10 minutes," which the Architect translates into swarm commands.

