Atomic oxygen (AO) durability. Based on MISSE flight data and ground testing, the AO erosion yield of POSS‑enhanced epoxy CFRP laminates is $1.67 \times 10^{-24}$ cm³ atom⁻¹ after 12‑month simulated LEO exposure (De Groh & Banks, 2020; recent MISSE‑9 through MISSE‑15 results confirm these yields). For a 15‑year mission at 500 km SSO, the total AO fluence is approximately $1.5 \times 10^{22}$ atoms cm⁻². With a protective SiO₂ overcoat (erosion yield two orders of magnitude lower), worst‑case mass loss is < 0.1 mm per exposed surface—well within structural margins.

4.5 Assembly Phases

All phases are fully autonomous.

1. T₀ — Nucleus + Scaffold. Dedicated Falcon 9 places the Nucleus and folded scaffold into 500 km SSO; scaffold inflates.
2. T+2 weeks — Swarm 1. Transporter rideshare deploys 60 tiles + 20 truss elements trailing the scaffold; tiles drift in over ~14 days.
3. T+8 weeks — Frame Closure. CBBA assignment + SCP trajectories + hierarchical consensus formation inside the scaffold; EM docking; E‑Walker welding of critical nodes.
4. T+24 weeks — Pressure Shell. Bladder inserted and inflated, or gaskets engaged; pressure test.
5. T+48 weeks — Utility Integration. Solar tiles, radiator tiles, PTMs dock autonomously; power/data mesh self‑configures.
6. T+72 weeks — Crew‑Ready. ECLSS (see §5.3) delivered by visit vehicle, robotically integrated; habitat pressurised and verified.

4.6 Human‑Swarm Interaction and Failure Modes

A supervisory tier allows the crew to query the swarm state and issue high‑level commands via natural language and a 3D digital twin. The top 10 failure modes are documented with probability, severity, detection method, and mitigation. Critical failures include: Nucleus AI failure (cold spare with automated failover); welder failure (crew EVA contingency); and bladder puncture (leak‑before‑crew detection, isolating bulkhead valve, crew‑applied patch).

