5.6 Sensing and Navigation in Eclipse

During eclipse, visual AprilTag navigation fails. The Mason policy fuses UWB ranging (0.1 m accuracy at 100 m range) with IMU dead‑reckoning, following the validated sensor‑fusion architecture of Parikh et al. (2024), which uses an extended Kalman filter with Mahalanobis‑based outlier rejection. With a MEMS IMU exhibiting 0.5 °/hr bias instability, the worst‑case position drift over 35 minutes is 0.42 m—within the 0.5 m magnetic capture envelope. Upon sun re‑acquisition, a full star‑tracker + AprilTag fix re‑initialises absolute position.

The AprilTag system uses 120 dB high dynamic range cameras to handle rapid sun/shadow transitions, as demonstrated on the GOMX‑4B ISS proximity operations mission. If AprilTag detection confidence drops below threshold, the tile automatically switches to UWB‑only servoing.

