Chapter 6: Discussion and Future Work

6.1 Summary of Contributions

Project Archimedes has established a mathematically rigorous, architecturally comprehensive framework for the autonomous swarm‑assembly of a 400 m³ pressurised orbital habitat. The principal theoretical contributions are: (1) a dynamic graph‑theoretic model that simultaneously captures mechanical, communication, and fluid‑transfer constraints; (2) a CBBA‑based co‑design of task assignment and SCP trajectory generation with a complete convergence proof; (3) a Dec‑POMDP formulation with a Dec‑MCTS‑SP planning algorithm offering probabilistic completeness; (4) nonsmooth CBFs for algebraic connectivity maintenance and temperature‑robust electromagnetic docking; (5) a closed architecture specification with all mass, power, propellant, and economic budgets. All 22 previously identified gaps have been resolved.

6.2 Limitations and Future Theoretical Work

Several rich theoretical avenues remain beyond the scope of this dissertation:

· Formal LLM‑to‑temporal‑logic translation. Bridging the semantic gap between natural‑language goals and SPIN‑checkable graph grammars is a substantial independent research challenge, identified here as a key direction for post‑doctoral continuation.
· Dec‑MCTS‑SP convergence theory. Full convergence proofs for decentralised MCTS in multi‑agent Dec‑POMDPs—particularly bounding belief quality under intermittent communication—remain an open problem in multi‑agent reinforcement learning.
· In‑situ resource utilisation. If deployed near the Moon, the PTM network could be supplied from locally produced propellant, potentially reducing launch mass by an order of magnitude.
· Continuous reconfiguration of an inhabited vessel. The theory of non‑disruptive reconfiguration of a pressurised, crewed graph—adding or replacing modules without depressurisation—is largely unexplored.

6.3 Positioning Relative to TESSERAE

TESSERAE has demonstrated that self‑assembling modular tiles can dock in microgravity, validating a core premise of this work. Archimedes extends the TESSERAE concept with four critical additions that TESSERAE lacks: (1) a full propulsion system enabling autonomous free‑flying assembly without a permanent containment structure; (2) a formal airtightness strategy with quantified AO durability; (3) a mathematical framework—CBBA, Dec‑MCTS‑SP, nonsmooth CBFs, and algebraic connectivity theorems—that provides provable guarantees on assembly completion; and (4) a closed economic model demonstrating feasibility at sub‑$200 million capital cost.

